---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for H-4 Families & Dependents | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for H-4 families explains how a primary investor’s petition can include a spouse and unmarried children under 21 as derivative green card beneficiaries.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-h4-families`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for H-4 Families & Dependents';
const subhead =
  'How EB-5 can offer stability for H-4 spouses and children facing work authorization uncertainty.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'H-1B to Green Card via EB-5',
    url: '/pathways/h1b-to-green-card',
    description: 'Employer-independence and concurrent filing framing for H-1B households.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 for Your H-4 Family"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for H-4 Families"
  keywords={['EB-5 for H-4', 'H-4 EAD EB-5', 'H-4 spouse green card', 'EB-5 derivative beneficiaries']}
>
  <h2>H-4 status and work authorization uncertainty</h2>
  <p>
    H-4 dependents are tied to the principal H-1B holder's status. H-4 EAD eligibility has been subject to policy
    challenges and processing variability, which leaves some spouses without reliable work authorization even when the
    household is otherwise stable. That uncertainty is one reason some families evaluate a self-sponsored green card path
    that does not depend on H-4 EAD rules remaining unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>How spouses and children can be included</h2>
  <p>
    When a qualifying principal investor files an EB-5 petition, a spouse and unmarried children under 21 can typically
    be included as derivative beneficiaries, subject to age-out and CSPA considerations that must be reviewed for each
    child. Derivative status does not create a separate investment requirement for each family member, but documentation,
    ages, and filing timing still matter. Families concerned about children approaching 21 should discuss CSPA timing with
    counsel before assuming coverage. For the H-1B principal's pathway framing, see
    <a href="/pathways/h1b-to-green-card">H-1B to green card via EB-5</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What EB-5 does — and does not — change overnight</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 is a separate petition process. It does not instantly convert H-4 status into permanent residence, and it does
    not guarantee work authorization on a fixed calendar. Concurrent filing, where available, may allow some applicants
    to seek an EAD while the case is pending — eligibility depends on status, category availability, and attorney review.
    For process mechanics, use the <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a> guide.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
